LABS={
'ecdf':('Latency, one task at a time','See the difference between a mean, a median, and a nearest-rank percentile.','Measured simulator data. Twelve completed tasks in each diagnostic run. These are not real-device benchmarks.', '''<div class="controls"><label>Diagnostic run<select name="run"><option value="after_fix_proposed">Proposed after repair</option><option value="fifo">FIFO</option><option value="before_fix_proposed">Proposed before repair</option><option value="forced_final_gpu">Forced final GPU</option><option value="after_fix_no_protection">No protection</option></select></label><label>Completion threshold <output>30 s</output><input aria-label="Completion threshold in seconds" name="threshold" type="range" min="0" max="60" value="30" step="1"></label></div><div class="metrics"></div><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 590 285" role="img" aria-label="Empirical cumulative distribution of task latency"></svg><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p><details><summary>Inspect all sorted observations</summary><table class="outputtable"><thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Latency (s)</th><th>Cumulative share</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></details>'''),
'paired':('Keep the workload pair','Change the scenario and baseline. Each line follows one workload across policies.','Measured simulator data, matched-service regime, three held-out seed pairs per scenario. These few pairs describe the tested workloads; they do not establish a population-level winner.', '''<div class="controls"><label>Scenario<select name="scenario"><option value="randomized">Randomized</option><option value="contention">Contention</option><option value="lookahead">Lookahead</option><option value="priority_fairness">Priority &amp; fairness</option></select></label><label>Baseline<select name="baseline"><option value="residency">Residency</option><option value="fifo">FIFO</option></select></label></div><div class="metrics"></div><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 590 270" role="img" aria-label="Paired makespans for three held-out workloads"></svg><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p><details><summary>Inspect the paired values</summary><table class="outputtable"><thead><tr><th>Seed</th><th>Baseline (s)</th><th>Proposed (s)</th><th>Difference (s)</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></details>'''),
'cohort':('When the average changes its story','B succeeds more often on both easy and hard tasks. Can you make its overall result look worse?','Synthetic teaching example. Easy-task success: A 90%, B 95%. Hard-task success: A 10%, B 20%. Sliders change only cohort composition, not either policy’s within-group performance.', '''<div class="controls"><label>Easy-task share for A <output for="shareA">91%</output><input name="shareA" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="91" step="1" aria-label="Easy-task percentage for A"></label><label>Easy-task share for B <output for="shareB">17%</output><input name="shareB" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="17" step="1" aria-label="Easy-task percentage for B"></label></div><button type="button">Give both the same mix</button><div class="metrics"></div><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 590 285" role="img" aria-label="Within-group and overall policy success rates"></svg><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p>'''),
'judge':('Agreement is only one denominator','Edit the confusion matrix and watch three apparently similar accuracy statements separate.','Synthetic labels from the companion, not actual human review. Rows with repeated trials are not independent tasks. False-positive rate = FP / (FP + TN); false-discovery fraction = FP / (TP + FP).', '''<div class="controls"><label>True positives (TP)<input name="tp" type="number" min="0" max="1000000" step="1" value="762"></label><label>False positives (FP)<input name="fp" type="number" min="0" max="1000000" step="1" value="85"></label></div><div class="controls"><label>False negatives (FN)<input name="fn" type="number" min="0" max="1000000" step="1" value="51"></label><label>True negatives (TN)<input name="tn" type="number" min="0" max="1000000" step="1" value="182"></label></div><button type="button">Restore companion example</button><div class="metrics"></div><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p>''')}
def lab(kind):
 title,intro,note,body=LABS[kind]
 return f'<section class="lab" data-lab="{kind}" id="lab-{kind}" aria-label="Interactive lab: {title}"><span class="eyebrow">Interactive lab · change one thing</span><h2>{title}</h2><p class="labintro">{intro}</p><noscript><p class="notice">Enable JavaScript to use this lab. The chapter and downloadable companion remain readable without it.</p></noscript>{body}<p class="labnote">{note}</p></section>'
LABS.update({
'vectors':('Projection and information loss','Rotate a direction or keep fewer principal components. Which information survives?', 'Synthetic chapter 14 examples. Projection uses x = (3, 1). PCA uses the four centered observations from the book; its axes are fixed for this dataset, with 80% and 20% of the variance. This is not a general PCA solver.', '''<div class="controls"><label>View<select name="view"><option value="projection">Vector projection</option><option value="pca">PCA reconstruction</option></select></label><label>Direction angle <output class="angleout">45°</output><input name="angle" aria-label="Projection angle in degrees" type="range" min="0" max="180" step="1" value="45"></label><label>Retained PCA components<select name="rank"><option value="0">0 · mean only</option><option value="1" selected>1 · leading direction</option><option value="2">2 · both directions</option></select></label></div><div class="metrics"></div><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 590 320" role="img" aria-label="Vector projection or PCA reconstruction plot"></svg><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p>'''),
'pixels':('A threshold becomes a measurement','Change the segmentation threshold and pixel calibration. Inspect the largest connected object.', 'The exact synthetic 64 × 64 image from chapter 15. Foreground means intensity greater than or equal to the threshold; components use four-neighbor connectivity. Area = selected pixels × spacing². This toy object is not a medical image or a validated measuring instrument.', '''<div class="controls"><label>Intensity threshold <output class="thresholdout">0.50</output><input name="threshold" aria-label="Image intensity threshold" type="range" min="0.1" max="0.9" step="0.01" value="0.5"></label><label>Pixel spacing (mm) <output class="spacingout">0.20</output><input name="spacing" aria-label="Square pixel spacing in millimeters" type="range" min="0.1" max="0.5" step="0.01" value="0.2"></label></div><div class="pixelpair"><div><canvas width="64" height="64" aria-label="Synthetic grayscale input image" role="img"></canvas><p>Observed intensity</p></div><div><canvas width="64" height="64" aria-label="Thresholded mask with largest component highlighted" role="img"></canvas><p>Green: largest component · gray: other foreground</p></div></div><div class="metrics"></div><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p>'''),
'gis':('A boundary changes the join','Move point C across the shared border and choose what it means to be inside.', 'Synthetic planar coordinates in kilometers from chapter 16, not latitude/longitude. Strict excludes all boundaries; closed includes them and can duplicate matches; half-open includes lower/left boundaries and excludes upper/right boundaries. The outer upper/right boundary is intentionally excluded in the half-open rule.', '''<div class="controls"><label>Boundary rule<select name="rule"><option value="half_open">Half-open</option><option value="strict">Strict interior</option><option value="closed">Closed / boundary included</option></select></label><label>Point C x-coordinate <output class="xout">2.0 km</output><input name="pointx" aria-label="Point C x coordinate in kilometers" type="range" min="1.5" max="2.5" value="2" step="0.1"></label></div><div class="metrics"></div><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 590 320" role="img" aria-label="Seven synthetic points over four rectangular regions"></svg><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p><details><summary>Inspect all point-to-region matches</summary><table class="outputtable"><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Region matches</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></details>'''),
'finance':('Discount cash flows, compound returns','Change a discount rate or return scale. Arithmetic averages do not describe compounded wealth.', 'Synthetic chapter 17 teaching data, not market observations or financial advice. Cash flows are −$10,000 now and equal savings at the end of each of the next three years. Returns are five ordered periods [+10%, −10%, +5%, −20%, +25%] times the selected scale. No taxes, fees, dividends, or additional cash flows are modeled.', '''<div class="controls"><label>Annual discount rate <output class="rateout">5%</output><input name="rate" aria-label="Annual discount rate percent" type="range" min="0" max="20" value="5" step="1"></label><label>Annual savings (USD) <output class="savingsout">4000</output><input name="savings" aria-label="Annual cash savings in dollars" type="range" min="3000" max="5000" value="4000" step="100"></label><label>Return scale <output class="scaleout">100%</output><input name="scale" aria-label="Return scale percent" type="range" min="0" max="200" value="100" step="10"></label></div><div class="metrics"></div><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 590 285" role="img" aria-label="Compounded wealth across five synthetic return periods"></svg><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p>'''),
'workers':('More workers, more overhead','Choose a worker budget and coordination cost. Inspect the best integer choice inside that budget.', 'Synthetic chapter 18 model: T(n) = 100/n + 2 + c(n − 1) seconds. The first term shrinks as work is split; the last grows with coordination. This deterministic sensitivity lab does not run new Monte Carlo draws. The chapter and companion separately analyze uncertain parameters.', '''<p class="formula">T(n) = 100/n + 2 + c(n − 1)</p><div class="controls"><label>Available worker budget <output class="budgetout">32</output><input name="budget" aria-label="Available worker budget" type="range" min="1" max="32" value="32" step="1"></label><label>Coordination c (s / additional worker) <output class="overheadout">0.20</output><input name="overhead" aria-label="Coordination seconds per additional worker" type="range" min="0.05" max="0.5" value="0.2" step="0.01"></label></div><div class="metrics"></div><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 590 285" role="img" aria-label="Predicted batch duration by worker count with feasible optimum highlighted"></svg><p class="results" aria-live="polite"></p>''')})
